# The Research Answer: Your Pyramid Is Correct, But Two of Your Instincts Need Correction

I read the primary sources. Your architecture — intelligent model at the top, cheap models executing, a divider layer that orthogonalizes tasks — is **exactly what the 2026 literature converges on**. But two of your assumptions are backwards, and knowing which two will save you from building the wrong thing.

---

## 1. The Economic Layer: Routing Is Not Optional, It Is 72% of Your Budget

Your instinct that "using a high-intelligence model for simple tasks is waste" is not just correct — it is **measurably the single largest cost lever available to you**.

| System | Mechanism | Measured result |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **RouteLLM** (Berkeley, ICLR 2025) | Learned router trained on preference data | **85% cost reduction** on MT-Bench at **95% of GPT-4 quality**, sending only **14% of queries** to the strong model |
| **FrugalGPT** (Stanford, 2023) | Cascade: cheap → expensive until confidence threshold | **Up to 98% cost reduction** at GPT-4 accuracy |
| **AgentRouter** (2026) | Per-step routing across 4 model tiers with trajectory-level signal | **72% cost reduction** at **97.3% of frontier quality** (<3% degradation) |

The critical finding that separates AgentRouter from the earlier two:

> *"RouteLLM and FrugalGPT (applied per-step) achieve only **31% and 44%** cost reduction respectively, because their **single-turn training signal misses trajectory-level quality dependencies**."*

**What this means for your pyramid:** you cannot route on the *prompt* alone. You must route on **where in the trajectory you are**. Planning steps need the strong model. The 8th file read in a batch does not. The router must be **state-aware**, not just query-aware.

**Your layer-1 model should be the router.** It knows the plan, knows what step is next, and can decide "this subtask is mechanical → Haiku / Gemma" vs "this subtask requires judgment → Sonnet / Opus". That decision is itself a model call — but a cheap one (~50 tokens of output) that saves 70% of the downstream cost.

