## 2. The Topology Layer: The 17.2× Number That Validates Your Entire Design

This is the most important finding in the whole answer, and it directly confirms your instinct about why the intelligent model must sit at the top.

A large-scale study of multi-agent scaling laws measured error propagation across topologies:

> *"Independent agents operating in parallel amplified errors **17.2 times**, while **centralized hierarchical oversight contained amplification to 4.4 times** but could not eliminate it."*

And the corollary that makes it actionable:

> *"Once a single agent gets past roughly **45% accuracy** on a task, adding more agents mostly adds two things: **coordination tax** and a lovely **17.2× error amplification effect**. In other words, if your base agent is mid, your swarm will be extremely mid."*

**Decomposed into what it means for your architecture:**

| Topology | Error amplification | What it looks like |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Single agent | 1.0× | Baseline |
| **Independent parallel agents** (no coordinator) | **17.2×** | A swarm where each worker does its own thing |
| **Centralized hierarchical** (your design) | **4.4×** | Planner at top, workers below, coordinator reconciles |
| Sequential reasoning (single-agent baseline) | — | Multi-agent variants degraded sequential reasoning by **39–70%** |

**Two conclusions you must build around:**

1. **Your pyramid is the 4.4× topology, not the 17.2× one.** The intelligent model at the top is not a luxury — it is the **error containment mechanism**. Remove it and error amplification quadruples.
2. **4.4× is not zero.** Centralized coordination *contains* but does not *eliminate* amplification. This means you must add **verification gates** at the reconcile step — the coordinator cannot blindly concatenate worker outputs.

The financial consequence is real: *"Poorly structured multi-agent systems can amplify errors up to 17 times and cost over **$500,000 a year** in extra tokens."* For a personal project that's absurd, but the mechanism is the same at any scale — a swarm without a coordinator burns tokens amplifying its own mistakes.

