## 3. Your Task-Divider Layer Already Has a Name and an Exact Algorithm

This is the part of your proposal I want to affirm most strongly. You said:

> *"The 2nd layer subagents need communication between themselves and they're not much intelligent to tackle the problem on their own by maintaining consistency between themselves, which causes ripple effect... so the context from the subagent — it divides into, arranges, assigns tasks by knowing the context to the later subagents."*

That is the **Orthogonal Partitioning** pattern, and it has four named invariants:

| Invariant | What it forbids | Why |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Disjoint Workspaces** | Two workers owning the same file / topic / data slice | *"When subagents share mutable state or write to the same files, coordination overhead and collision resolution **eliminate the benefits of concurrency**"* |
| **Single Writer Principle** | Two workers authorized to write to the same resource | Enforced at the registry level, not by convention |
| **Staged Output** | Workers writing directly to the shared artifact | Workers write to `staging/task_*.md` or discrete modules; the coordinator reconciles in a **Reduce phase** |
| **Context Cleanliness** | Passing the parent's full history to a worker | *"Disposable workers run with a **clean context window per task**, preventing prompt dilution, tool-log pollution, and cognitive drift"* |

**The structural flow is exactly your pyramid:**

