## 6. Schema Registry: The Research Answer Is "Shared Registry With Eviction, Not Broadcast"

Your instinct that subagents need a **schema registry** for consistency is correct, and there is a specific mechanism for it.

**RAMPART** (Registry-based Agentic Memory with Priority-Aware Runtime Transformation) describes exactly the control-plane problem you identified:

> *"In the shared-registry model, a coordinating registry can **revoke a subagent's access to a tool by evicting the schema block before the subagent's next compile call, with no round-trip required**."*

This is the key: **the registry is the permission system**. A subagent does not get a "copy" of a tool schema — it gets a **reference into the shared registry**, and the coordinator can revoke that reference mid-run by removing the schema block. That is how you prevent a compromised or drifting subagent from continuing to use a tool the coordinator has decided it should no longer have.

**The cross-protocol problem is also named:**

> *"Descriptors in MCP (tools), A2A (roles/behaviors), and ACP (message schemas/policies) use **different primitives and evolve at different speeds**... cross-protocol operation requires a **shared metadata core**."*

**AgentHub** proposes SBOM-style manifests that express *"runtime permissions, preconditions, environment bindings, and protocol roles"*.

**And the subagent registry pattern is already implemented in one harness:**

> *"Subagent registries adopt it so their shell work is torn down with the parent thread on DeleteThread... Because **schemas and execution share this registry**, delegate_task and resume_task are **neither advertised to the model nor dispatchable** by that runtime."*

This is the recursion guard: **the subagent's tool registry does not contain `AgentTool`**. The model cannot spawn a child because the tool is not in its schema. That is how you enforce the depth limit — **structurally, not by policy**.

**Your schema registry therefore needs four things:**

| Component | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Shared tool registry** | One source of truth for all tool schemas, referenced (not copied) by every agent |
| **Per-subagent filtered registry** | The coordinator passes a **subset** of the parent registry; subagents cannot see tools they are not authorized for |
| **Schema eviction** | Coordinator can revoke a tool mid-run by removing it from the subagent's registry |
| **Recursion sentinel** | `AgentTool` is **absent** from subagent registries beyond depth N |

