## 7. Communication: Pub/Sub for Events, Blackboard for State, Never Both for the Same Data

You asked whether to use "redis pubsub events between the subagents like microservices." The research answer is **nuanced** — there are two distinct patterns, and conflating them is the failure mode.

### Pattern A: Blackboard (shared state, read/write by role)

> *"All agents run in one process and **synchronize through one shared blackboard (single source of truth)**... stronger long-horizon behavior on short-context models through **role-isolated working memory**."*

> *"Blackboard-Core provides a centralized state architecture... Instead of agents messaging each other directly, **all agents read from and write to a shared Blackboard (state), while a Supervisor LLM orchestrates which agent runs next**... Branch-Merge — Fork states, work in isolation, merge results."*

And the architectural framing that matches your proposal exactly:

> *"The essence of multi-agent collaboration is not necessarily agents chatting with each other, but **division of labor over reading and writing the same state**."*

### Pattern B: Message Bus (events, task queues, notifications)

> *"Fabric A2A... Async Messaging — **Redis Streams for persistent task queues**. Real-time Events — **Pub/Sub for broadcasts and notifications**. Agent Registry — PostgreSQL-backed discovery service. ACL Security — fine-grained permissions per agent."*

And the protocol-level standard for delegation:

> *"A2A uses HTTP, SSE, and JSON-RPC 2.0 — with **Agent Cards for capability discovery** and **Tasks for work delegation**... Task lifecycle: submitted, working, input-required, completed, failed."*

### The reconciliation — when to use which

The research gives a clear decision rule:

| Use RPC (A2A/MCP) when... | Use pub/sub when... |
| :--- | :--- |
| Low-latency point operation | **Broadcast queries** |
| Immediate feedback required | **Coordinated multi-agent actions** |
| **Well-defined error contracts** | **Efficient collection of results in real time** |

**For your architecture:**

| Data | Pattern | Why |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **The plan / todo list** | **Blackboard** (read by all, written only by coordinator) | Single source of truth; workers need to read it, never write it |
| **Task dispatch** | **A2A RPC** (`fabric.call`) | Point-to-point, needs error contract, needs result |
| **Task completion events** | **Pub/Sub** | Broadcast to coordinator and any observers |
| **Tool registry** | **Shared registry with eviction** (RAMPART) | Not a message — a reference with revocable access |
| **Worker staged output** | **Isolated files, read by coordinator at reduce** | Never through the bus — files are the buffer |

**The anti-pattern to avoid:** workers **writing to the plan**. If two workers can write to the shared todo list, you have shared mutable state and the 17.2× amplification returns. **Only the coordinator writes the plan.**

